%description:

RFC 5494 -- a packet with a hardware space reserved for experiments provokes nothing.

Checks RFC826-RECV-2 from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc826/catalog.md and
RFC5494-NUM-2 from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc5494/catalog.md. The English procedure
is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/arp/checks/input-validation.md#an-experimental-hardware-space;
the program steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the shared link
mockup: host A = hostA, host B = hostB, host C = hostC, switch S = switch.

Every field of the crafted packet is that of a valid request from host A to host B, except
the hardware space, which is 36 -- HW_EXP1 of RFC 5494 section 3. The hardware length stays
6 and the target protocol address is the address of host B, so the only reason to discard the
packet is the one under test: the first question of the reception algorithm of RFC 826, "Do
I have the hardware type in ar$hrd?".

The packet is built octet by octet and not from field values. The hardware space is not a
field of the packet class of the simulation model, which carries the value implicitly, so
only the octets can hold a value other than the one for an Ethernet.

Defect. This test FAILS and the failure is NOT declared expected, because the model claims the
behaviour: ArpPacketSerializer::deserializeFields tests this very field and calls markIncorrect
when it does not hold (ArpPacketSerializer.cc:52 to 55). So the model detects the condition RFC 826
asks it to detect, and what it does next is wrong -- Arp::processArpPacket peeks the packet with no
flags and the run stops. A mechanism that exists and does the wrong thing is a defect.

What the run shows. The model reads the octets with ArpPacketSerializer, whose
deserializeFields marks the packet incorrect when the hardware space is not 1
(src/inet/networklayer/arp/ipv4/ArpPacketSerializer.cc), and Arp::processArpPacket then
peeks the packet, which stops the run with
"Returning an incorrect chunk is not allowed according to the flags: 0".
RFC 826 asks for a discard and an end of processing, and the run never reaches the end.
See doc/project/evidence/model/arp/results.md.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "ArpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// HW_EXP1, the first hardware space value that RFC 5494 section 3 reserves for experiments.
static const uint16_t HW_EXP1 = 36;

static Packet *buildExperimentalHardwareSpacePacket(const CaptureStore&)
{
    return buildRawArpPacket("craftedHardwareSpace", "hostB.eth[0]",
            HW_EXP1, ETHERTYPE_IPv4, 6, 4, 1,
            interfaceMacAddress("hostA.eth[0]"), interfaceIpv4Address("hostA.eth[0]"),
            MacAddress::UNSPECIFIED_ADDRESS, interfaceIpv4Address("hostB.eth[0]"));
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc5494_experimental_hardware_space")
        // Expected observation 1: the crafted packet arrives at host B, hardware space 36.
        .inject(inject("hostB").into("eth[0]", "upperLayerOut").at(0.5)
                  .describe("an ARP packet with the experimental hardware space 36, addressed to host B")
                  .packet(buildExperimentalHardwareSpacePacket))
        // Expected observations 2 and 3: host B puts no ARP packet on the link afterwards,
        // and host B goes on working to the end of the run (RFC826-RECV-2, RECV-1).
        .never(on("hostB")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return arpPacketLeaves(c.event, "hostB"); })
                  .describe("any ARP packet from host B")
                  .within(0.4));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc5494_experimental_hardware_space compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.ethernet.EthernetSwitch;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The shared link mockup: three hosts on one Ethernet segment, joined by a switch.
network ArpSharedNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        switch: EthernetSwitch { @display("p=400,150"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=250,250"); }
        hostC: StandardHost { @display("p=550,150"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostB.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostC.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = ArpSharedNet
sim-time-limit = 1s
seed-set = 0

# No host sends a datagram: the crafted packet is the only stimulus of the run.

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc5494_experimental_hardware_space: PASS
